<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="building-and-testing" title="Building and testing">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Needs JDK %jdk% and Maven %maven%. The enforcer stops the build on anything older.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        One command builds the Maven modules and runs every check in the reactor.
    </p>

    <procedure title="Build everything" id="all">
        <step>
            <p>Run it from the repository root.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
mvn -B clean verify
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>A green run ends with a reactor summary, ten modules and one verdict.</p>
            <code-block lang="console">
[INFO] BUILD SUCCESS
            </code-block>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <tip>
        <p>
            Add <code>-o</code> to hold Maven offline. Nothing in the reactor needs the network
            once your local repository has the build's plugins. On a first clone it fails.
        </p>
    </tip>

    <chapter title="What verify runs" id="what">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    subgraph v["validate"]
        A["Enforcer"] --> B["Checkstyle"]
    end
    subgraph t["test"]
        C["Surefire, each module's unit suite"] --> D["Architecture tests, over import lines and doc comments"]
    end
    subgraph f["verify"]
        E["Javadoc, resolving every link"]
    end
    v --> t --> f
        </code-block>

        <p>
            A doc comment answers to both ends: the tests decide whether it exists, the javadoc
            goal whether its links resolve.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Run less than all of it" id="narrow">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>What you changed</td>
                <td>What to run</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Code in one module</td>
                <td><code>mvn -B -o -pl aether-weaver-engine -am test</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Imports or formatting</td>
                <td><code>mvn -B -o validate</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A doc comment</td>
                <td><code>mvn -B -o verify</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A page of this site</td>
                <td><code>python3 build-config/docsite/check-docs.py --build</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Anything, before you push</td>
                <td><code>mvn -B -o clean verify</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            <code>-am</code> builds what the module you named depends on. Leave it off and Maven
            takes those modules from your local repository. That is whatever you last installed.
            <a href="artifacts.topic#graph">What depends on what</a> has the direction the arrows
            point in.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Build the IntelliJ IDEA plugin" id="ide">
        <p>
            <code>aether-weaver-ide</code> is a Gradle build. Its doc comments are still checked
            by <code>verify</code>.
            <a href="repository-layout.topic">Repository layout</a> has what working on it costs.
        </p>
        <step>
            <p>Install the artefacts it resolves from your local repository.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
mvn -B -o -pl aether-weaver-processor -am install -DskipTests
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Run the checks that need no running IDE.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
cd aether-weaver-ide/aether-weaver-idea
./gradlew checkstyleMain checkstyleTest checkstyleSample javadoc compileSample
            </code-block>
            <note>
                <p>
                    The IntelliJ distribution sits on the compile classpath. On a cold cache this
                    step downloads a full IDE.
                </p>
            </note>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Gradle prints the verdict once the tasks finish.</p>
            <code-block lang="console">
BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 1s
11 actionable tasks: 1 executed, 10 up-to-date
            </code-block>
            <p>
                Above it, an incubating problems report and a deprecation warning about
                Gradle 10. Below it, an offer of the configuration cache. All three are
                expected.
            </p>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="Two Gradle tasks that go further" id="gradle-extra">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="./gradlew check">
                <p>
                    Starts the platform in-process for the fixture tests. It also runs
                    <code>checkSampleVersion</code>, the one failure step 2 skips. The sample pins
                    its own <code>aether-weaver</code> version, and this catches it drifting.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="./gradlew verifyPlugin">
                <p>
                    Runs JetBrains' Plugin Verifier against the releases <code>recommended()</code>
                    resolves, downloading each. The plugin sets no upper build. It claims more
                    than any run covers.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What CI adds" id="ci">
        <p>
            A green local build is most of the gate, not all of it.
            <path>.github/workflows/build.yml</path> runs five jobs.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>The same <code>verify</code>, on Ubuntu, Windows and macOS. Class files are
                discovered by walking directories, and separators differ.</p></li>
            <li><p>Two clean packages, compared jar by jar. A timestamp or a
                <code>HashMap</code> iteration destroys reproducibility silently.</p></li>
            <li><p>One <code>verify</code> under <code>tr-TR</code> and <code>Asia/Tokyo</code>.
                Diagnostics are formatted text, and formatting is locale-sensitive.</p></li>
            <li><p>One run on the next JDK, as an early-access build. It is allowed to fail.</p></li>
            <li><p>The Gradle build, with both tasks above. Skipping those two locally is
                safe.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="When it goes red" id="red">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Checkstyle violations, before anything compiles">
                <p>
                    A line over 120 characters, a star import, an unused import, or a tab.
                    The plugin reads the same file, so a rule holding here holds there.
                    <a href="repository-layout.topic">Repository layout</a> has the rules.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="ASM, Javassist, Byte Buddy and cglib are banned">
                <p>
                    The enforcer found one on a classpath, and it counts test scope. Take the
                    dependency out and use <code>java.lang.classfile</code>.
                    <a href="artifacts.topic">Artefacts and modules</a> has the one exclusion.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A test in the architecture package failed">
                <p>
                    Your change crossed a module boundary, or left a declaration undocumented.
                    The usual cause is an import added only to satisfy a <code>{@link}</code>.
                    <a href="repository-layout.topic">Repository layout</a> covers the first, and
                    the <a href="javadoc-policy.topic">JavaDoc policy</a> the second.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The javadoc goal failed at verify">
                <p>
                    Doclint resolved a reference to nothing, or found HTML it could not parse.
                    Correct the name, or drop the tag. Doclint reports as a warning, and the goal
                    fails on warnings.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

